---
/**
 * What a business gets from the guide, and what it can buy.
 *
 * Every place page ends with "Own or run X?", and the answer to the question
 * that follows — "so what does that get me, and what does it cost?" — lived
 * nowhere. This is the page: a free listing is editorial and stays that
 * way; the things for sale sit beside the editorial and are labeled; and
 * here is how to claim, correct, or sponsor. Written for the owner of a
 * café, not for a media buyer, who has /advertise/ on the hub.
 */
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro';
import CtaBand from '@/components/CtaBand.astro';
import TipsLink from '@/components/TipsLink.astro';
import { getNetworkHub, getTown } from '@/config';
import { getTownEntries } from '@/lib/content';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd } from '@/lib/seo';
import { LAND_TYPES } from '@/content/schemas';

const town = getTown();
const hub = getNetworkHub();
const places = (await getTownEntries('places')).filter((p) => !p.delisted);
const businesses = places.filter((p) => !LAND_TYPES.includes(p.data.type)).length;
// The directory counts every place, parks and trails included; this page is
// for the businesses among them, and says both so the two pages agree.
const land = places.length - businesses;
// With no form configured the contact page falls back to a mailto link, so
// the claim link and the sentence about it say that instead of promising a
// pre-filled form that is not there.
const hasForm = Boolean(town.formspreeId);
const claim = hasForm ? `/contact/?about=${encodeURIComponent('My business')}#message-form` : '/contact/';
const partner = `mailto:${hub.social.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(`Partnership — ${town.siteTitle}`)}`;
const description = `How ${town.siteTitle} lists businesses in ${town.name}, ${town.state}: listings are free and editorial; sponsorship is labeled and sits beside them. How to claim or correct a listing.`;
---
<Page
  title="For businesses"
  seoTitle={`For businesses in ${town.name}: listings, corrections and sponsorship`}
  eyebrow={town.siteTitle}
  {description}
  intro={`If you run something in ${town.name}, this is how the guide works for you: what a listing is, how to fix one, and what is for sale.`}
  jsonLd={breadcrumbJsonLd(town, [
    { name: town.name, path: '/' },
    { name: 'For businesses', path: '/for-businesses/' },
  ])}
  narrow
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A listing is free, and it is ours</h2>
    <p>
      {town.siteTitle} lists {businesses} businesses and venues in {town.name}{land > 0 && <Fragment>, and {land} parks and trails beside them, {places.length} places in all</Fragment>}, because they are here, not because
      anyone paid. Nobody can buy a listing, buy a better position, or buy their way out of one. Each entry is
      checked against a source — usually your own website — and says when it was last checked. That is what makes
      readers trust the page you are on, and that trust is the whole point of being on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A listing carries your name, what you are, where you are, when you are open, your phone number and your
      website, a sentence or two on what you are known for, and a photograph if we have one we are allowed to use.
      It also shows whether you are open right now, worked out from the hours on the page, and it appears in the
      <a href="/directory/">directory</a>, in the <a href="/search/">site search</a>, and beside any event held at
      your address.
    </p>

    <h2>Claim it, correct it, add to it</h2>
    <p>
      The fastest way to improve your listing is to <a href={claim}>write to us from it</a>. Every place page has an
      “Own or run this?” link at the foot of its details, which {hasForm ? 'opens the form with your name already filled in' : 'starts an email to us with your name in the subject line'}.
      Things that are always welcome:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hours.</strong> One line, in your words: “Tue–Sat 10–5; closed Sun–Mon.” We keep it in that shape so the site can tell readers when you are open.</li>
      <li><strong>A photograph</strong> you own or have the right to give us, with who to credit. Most listings have none, and a listing with one is read more.</li>
      <li><strong>A correction.</strong> A phone number, an address, a closed location, a change of name. <TipsLink class="" />.</li>
      <li><strong>An event.</strong> Anything open to the public — a tasting, a class, a market day — goes on the events page for nothing. <a href="/submit-event/">Submit it here</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Not listed at all? Say so on the same form. If you are in {town.name} and open to the public, you belong here.</p>

    <h2>What is for sale</h2>
    <p>
      Sponsorship, labeled as such, beside the editorial and never inside it. A sponsor’s name appears on a
      page or in the weekly email as “presented by”, with a link. It does not change a listing, the order of a
      list, or what we write. The placements on this guide are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A “presented by” line on the <a href="/events/">events page</a> and the <a href="/this-weekend/">weekend page</a> — the pages readers come back to every week.</li>
      <li>A labeled block on <a href="/moving-here/">Moving Here</a>, which is where people about to move to {town.name} look for an agent, a lender, a mover.</li>
      {hub.newsletter && <li>A line at the top of the weekly {town.siteTitle} email.</li>}
    </ul>
    <p>
      One sponsor per placement, so a sponsor is not competing with three others on the same page. Prices and
      audience figures are on the network’s <a href={`https://${hub.domain}/advertise/`}>advertising page</a>, which
      only quotes numbers it has actually measured. To talk about a placement, <a href={partner}>email us</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What we will not do</h2>
    <p>
      Write a listing you dictate, take a listing down because a competitor asked, call a sponsor “our pick”, or
      put an advertisement where a reader would take it for a recommendation. The <a href="/editorial/">editorial
      policy</a> says the same at more length, and it applies to us as much as to you.
    </p>
  </div>
  {/* The page's actions were all inline links; these give it a foot. */}
  <CtaBand
    slot="after"
    eyebrow="For businesses"
    title="Something to add or fix?"
    links={[
      { label: 'Correct a listing', href: '/correct/', primary: true },
      { label: 'Submit an event', href: '/submit-event/' },
    ]}
  />
</Page>
